**The required sight distance and optical analyses (Study 4) are not the subject of this study.** The resulting projected-speed profiles from chapter 4 are the input for those analyses (P~zp~ = f(V~p~)).

The numerical calculation is given in tabular form in this study (appendices A) and in the accompanying workbook (Excel), in which all steps are done with formulas, and all diagrams in this study are made as Excel charts that recalculate automatically when new printouts are put into the *Ulaz_n* sheets (input data are marked in blue).

Za određivanje brzine V85 primijenjena j:
To determine the V85 speed, the method from the **Rulebook on the conditions that public roads outside settlements must meet from the aspect of traffic safety (Official Gazette of RS 50/2011), Annex 2** [1] was applied, together with the **Manual for the design of roads in the Republic of Serbia (Putevi Srbije), part 4.0** [2] (driving model). According to these documents the projected speed V~p~ is not given in advance but is obtained as a *consequence* of the adopted geometry elements, in the range

$$V_{ri} \le V_p \le \max V_r, \qquad \max V_r = V_r + <<dm>> = <<mx>>\ \text{km/h}$$

The maximum speed on a tangent is max V~r~, i.e. V~ri~ + <<dm>> km/h, and this value is compared with max V~r~ from Table 3-04 of Annex 2 and must not exceed it<<tab34>>.

The resulting profile V~p~ is constructed along the chainage as follows:

1. for each circular curve the speed that the driver achieves in the curve is determined from the **V~p~ – R** relationship; on tangents the target speed is max V~r~;
2. from the **V~p~ – i~N~** relationship (influence of the longitudinal grade) the target speed is determined as a function of the grade of the grade line in the direction of travel;
3. the governing target speed is the smaller of these two (and of a possible speed limit in the scenario);
4. the target speed is "smoothed" by a driving model with acceleration a = <<a>> m/s² and deceleration d = <<d>> m/s² (nominally a = d = 0.8 m/s², with the distance of speed change determined by the formula from Figure 3-09: L = 0.1·V~avg~·ΔV, which corresponds to a = d = 0.77 m/s²);
5. on the profile obtained in this way the statistical indicators (V~p,avg~, σ, D~h~) and the consistency of adjacent elements ΔV~p~ are determined.

The profile is constructed separately for each direction of travel: in the direction of increasing chainage and in the direction of decreasing chainage (the longitudinal grade i~N~ is taken with its sign in the direction of travel, + uphill). Cases (sections) in the same direction of travel are chained at segment boundaries: the speed at the end of one section is the entry speed of the next (both acceleration and deceleration are satisfied across the boundary). At the entrance to the alignment free driving at <<ve>> km/h is assumed.

**Note on the relationships.** The V~p~ – R and V~p~ – i~N~ relationships are given graphically in Annex 2 of the Rulebook [1] (Figures 3-07 and 3-08): (a) for V~p~ – R, linear interpolation of the table of minimum radii R~min~(V~ri~) was applied, calculated by the formula from Figure 3-07, R~min~ = V~ri~² / (127·(f~R~(V) + i~pk,max~)), with i~pk,max~ = 7 % and f~R~ from Table 3-05, limited to max V~r~; (b) for V~p~ – i~N~ the table was read from the diagram in Figure 3-08 (reading accuracy about ±5 km/h) [4]. Both relationships are input data in the workbook (sheet *Parametri*) and can be replaced by more precisely read values without changing the other formulas. The results of chapter 4 depend on these tables, so checking the read values against the original diagrams is recommended.
